<TopicPage slug="subjects/computer-science/foundational/computer-architecture-and-hardware">

  <h2>Where you are, and what you will learn</h2>
  <p>
    This is a foundational lesson of Computer Science — the hardware half of the subject's
    first section, computer systems. You need no prior lesson: if you have ever used a
    laptop, phone or games console, you have operated the machine this lesson opens up.
    Everyday arithmetic is enough; binary numbers help but are rebuilt where needed.
    The lesson follows the Cambridge IGCSE Computer Science 0478 syllabus, Section 3
    (Hardware): computer architecture, input and output devices, data storage, and network
    hardware.
  </p>
  <p>By the end you will be able to:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>describe the von Neumann architecture — stored programs, CPU, memory, buses — and name the registers and the ALU and control unit;</li>
    <li>walk through the fetch–decode–execute cycle, saying what moves along which bus at each stage;</li>
    <li>compare RAM and ROM, and explain why a computer needs both;</li>
    <li>classify input and output devices, and explain how scanners, laser printers and 3D printers work;</li>
    <li>compare magnetic, optical and solid-state storage in speed, capacity, cost and reliability;</li>
    <li>state the job of each piece of network hardware — NIC, hub, switch, bridge, repeater, router, gateway — and choose the right device for a job.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>The idea: one machine, one memory, one endless cycle</h2>
  <p>
    Open the case of any computer from the last seventy years and the same skeleton is
    inside: a processor that calculates, a memory that holds, wires that connect, and
    devices at the edges. The design is called the <strong>von Neumann architecture</strong>,
    after the mathematician John von Neumann, whose 1945 report described a machine with one
    decisive idea — the <strong>stored-program concept</strong>: instructions are stored in
    the same memory as data, written in the same binary language. Before this idea, reprogramming
    a computer meant rewiring it for days; after it, changing what the machine does means
    loading new instructions into memory. Every phone, laptop, server and smart washing
    machine is this design. The CPU (central processing unit) is the part that runs the
    cycle: it fetches an instruction from memory, decodes what it means, executes it, and
    repeats — billions of times a second. Hardware is the study of how that skeleton is
    built, and of everything hanging off it.
  </p>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> "the CPU is the whole computer — the box under the desk
  is 'the CPU'." It seems reasonable because shop assistants say it and the box is the
  biggest thing in sight. But the CPU is one chip a few centimetres across; the box is the
  case, which contains the CPU plus memory, storage, power supply, cooling and ports. The
  correct picture: think of the case as the building, the CPU as one hyperactive office
  worker in it, memory as the desk surface they work on, and storage as the filing
  cabinets down the hall. A contrasting example: your phone has a CPU (actually several
  cores on one chip) and no "box" at all — the case-equals-CPU confusion evaporates the
  moment you notice the whole computer fits behind a sheet of glass.</p>

  <h2>Inside the CPU: registers, ALU, control unit, buses</h2>
  <p>
    The CPU contains a handful of named parts, and the names matter because the exam asks
    for them and because each has one job. The <strong>arithmetic and logic unit (ALU)</strong>
    does the mathematics and the comparisons: addition, subtraction, AND, OR, shifts, tests
    for zero. The <strong>control unit (CU)</strong> coordinates everything: it decodes
    instructions and sends the control signals that tell memory, the ALU and devices what
    to do and when. The <strong>registers</strong> are tiny, extremely fast storage cells
    inside the CPU, each with a dedicated role: the <strong>program counter (PC)</strong>
    holds the address of the next instruction; the <strong>memory address register (MAR)</strong>
    holds the address currently being read from or written to; the <strong>memory data
    register (MDR)</strong> holds the data just read or about to be written; the <strong>current
    instruction register (CIR)</strong> holds the instruction being decoded; the
    <strong>accumulator</strong> holds the ALU's running result. The parts are connected by
    <strong>buses</strong> — shared wires with a direction contract: the <strong>address
    bus</strong> carries addresses one way, from CPU to memory; the <strong>data bus</strong>
    carries the data itself, both ways; the <strong>control bus</strong> carries the CU's
    commands and the responses ("read now", "write now", "ready"). A wider address bus can
    name more memory locations — 16 address lines reach 2 to the power 16 = 65,536
    locations, which is the n-bits-give-2-to-the-n rule from the binary lesson, wearing
    hardware.
  </p>

  <h2>The fetch–decode–execute cycle</h2>
  <p>
    Everything the computer does — every game frame, every search, every keystroke echoed —
    reduces to one loop, paced by the system clock. The three stages and their traffic are
    worth walking slowly, because they are the machine.
  </p>
  <Widget no="1" caption="fetch, decode, execute — the loop every processor runs forever" widget="equationStep" config={fdeCfg} />
  <p>
    <strong>What to notice:</strong> two registers only ever talk to memory (MAR sends
    addresses, MDR carries data), and the PC is incremented <em>during</em> fetch, before
    execution even starts — which is exactly how the machine knows where to go next without
    being told. Notice too that "jump" instructions overwrite the PC at the execute stage:
    that single move is what lets programs loop, branch and call functions. The cycle is
    simple; its speed is the miracle.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>So far:</strong> the stored-program machine runs one loop — fetch, decode,
    execute — across a CPU of ALU, control unit and registers, connected to memory by
    address, data and control buses. The loop needs somewhere to fetch from, so now the two
    kinds of onboard memory.
  </p>

  <h2>RAM and ROM: the two onboard memories</h2>
  <p>
    A computer keeps two kinds of primary memory, and the difference is the first exam
    question hardware asks. <strong>RAM</strong> (random access memory) is the working
    memory: programs and their data are loaded into it while they run. It is fast, can be
    read and written freely, and is <strong>volatile</strong> — power off, contents gone.
    <strong>ROM</strong> (read-only memory) is fixed: it is <strong>non-volatile</strong>,
    keeps its contents without power, and in ordinary operation is only read. Its job is to
    hold the <strong>bootstrap</strong> (the startup firmware, the program the CPU runs the
    instant power arrives, which finds the storage device, loads the operating system into
    RAM and hands over). The division of labour follows: power on, CPU runs ROM's bootstrap,
    operating system loads into RAM, your applications load into RAM, and everything you are
    currently editing lives in RAM until saved. Why both? RAM is fast but forgets; ROM
    remembers but is fixed and slow to change. The computer needs a permanent home for its
    first instructions (ROM) and a large, fast scratchpad (RAM) — the same reason a kitchen
    has both a recipe card nailed to the wall and a clearable worktop.
  </p>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> "ROM is where your files are kept, because it is
  'read-only memory'." It seems reasonable from the name and the fact that files feel
  permanent. But ROM holds only the machine's startup firmware — a few megabytes at most;
  your files live on <em>secondary storage</em> (a hard drive or SSD), which is read-write
  and non-volatile but a different device entirely. The correct picture: three tiers, three
  roles — ROM (fixed startup instructions), RAM (fast, volatile workspace), secondary
  storage (large, permanent filing). The contrasting example: pulling a computer's power
  cable destroys everything in RAM instantly, while the files on the SSD survive untouched —
  and the machine still boots afterwards, because ROM never lost anything.</p>

  <h2>Input and output devices</h2>
  <p>
    Devices are how the digital world meets the physical one. <strong>Input devices</strong>
    convert human or environmental information into binary data: keyboard, mouse, touch
    screen; <strong>2D scanners</strong>, which sweep light across a page and record the
    reflection with an array of light sensors to produce an image file (and, with
    optical character recognition, editable text); <strong>3D scanners</strong>, which
    sweep a laser or structured light over an object and triangulate thousands of surface
    points into a 3D model used in CAD, prosthetics fitting and heritage preservation;
    <strong>barcode and QR readers</strong>, which decode printed patterns; microphones;
    and <strong>sensors</strong> — temperature, pressure, light, humidity, infrared, sound,
    pH — the input devices of automatic systems, feeding measurements to a computer that
    acts without a human. <strong>Output devices</strong> convert data back into light,
    sound or matter: screens; <strong>speakers</strong>; <strong>actuators</strong>, which
    turn control signals into physical motion (a valve, a motor, a heater — the output half
    of every sensor-driven system); and printers. <strong>Laser printers</strong> build a
    page electrostatically: a drum is given charge, a laser discharges the image onto it,
    powdered toner sticks to the charged areas, transfers to paper, and is fused by heated
    rollers — fast, sharp text, cheap per page, ideal for offices. <strong>Inkjet
    printers</strong> fire microscopic droplets of liquid ink directly onto the page —
    slower and costlier per page, but better for photo colour on many papers and cheaper
    to buy. <strong>3D printers</strong> output matter, not marks: a nozzle melts plastic
    filament and lays it down layer after layer (additive manufacturing) until a physical
    object exists — the output device that makes the prototype. Touch screens, incidentally,
    are both device types at once: input where you touch, output where you look.
  </p>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> "a scanner reads text the way it reads pictures — you
  scan a page and get a document you can edit." It seems reasonable because the scanned
  file looks exactly like the page. But a 2D scanner only ever produces an <em>image</em> —
  a bitmap of coloured dots; the letters are ink shapes, not characters. The correct
  picture: to get editable text, the scanned image must be processed by <strong>optical
  character recognition (OCR)</strong> software, which classifies each shape as a letter
  and makes mistakes with faint print and unusual fonts — which is why OCR output always
  needs proofreading. The contrast: QR codes are designed for machines rather than being
  read from them, with built-in error correction — a QR reader decodes them near-perfectly,
  while OCR of human prose is a statistical best guess.</p>

  <p>
    <strong>So far:</strong> the machine computes in the CPU, remembers temporarily in RAM,
    permanently in ROM, and talks to the world through input and output devices — now comes
    the third memory role: holding everything when the power is off.
  </p>

  <h2>Data storage: magnetic, optical, solid state</h2>
  <p>
    Secondary storage holds data permanently, and three technologies compete on four axes:
    capacity, speed, cost per gigabyte and reliability. <strong>Magnetic storage</strong>
    (the hard disk drive, HDD) spins platters coated in magnetic material past a read-write
    head: enormous capacities and the lowest cost per gigabyte, but mechanical — the head
    must physically move, so access is slow (milliseconds) and drops and vibration are
    dangerous. <strong>Optical storage</strong> (CD, DVD, Blu-ray) writes and reads pits in
    a reflective disc with a laser: the finer the laser's wavelength, the smaller the pits —
    CD's infrared laser gives about 700 MB, DVD's red laser about 4.7 GB per layer, Blu-ray's
    blue-violet laser 25 GB per layer. Optical discs are cheap and portable, and useful for
    distribution, but slow and small by modern standards. <strong>Solid-state storage</strong>
    (the SSD, USB memory stick, SD card) stores bits as trapped charge in flash memory
    cells: no moving parts at all — fast (dozens of times quicker than an HDD at typical
    tasks), silent, shockproof and light, at the highest cost per gigabyte, with the quirk
    that each cell survives a limited number of write cycles, managed invisibly by wear-
    levelling. The modern default follows from the axes: SSDs for the operating system and
    programs (speed), HDDs for bulk archives (cost), the cloud for backup (a copy held off
    site). Watch the axes move in the widget below.
  </p>
  <Widget no="2" caption="size divided by speed — what a copy actually costs in waiting" widget="sliderLab" config={storageCfg} />
  <p>
    <strong>What to notice:</strong> the first two readouts are one arithmetic sentence —
    time is size divided by speed — and the third shows the punchline of solid-state
    storage: multiply the speed by ten and the wait divides by ten. Then compare honestly
    with your own machine: an 8,000 MB game update at an old HDD's effective 100 MB/s costs
    about 1.3 minutes; at a fast SSD's 2,000 MB/s, four seconds. Same data, same capacity —
    different experience, which is exactly why the speed axis, not the capacity axis,
    decided the last decade of hardware.
  </p>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> "a bigger drive makes the computer faster — my machine
  slowed down, so I need more gigabytes." It seems reasonable because capacity numbers
  dominate the adverts. But capacity and speed are separate axes: a 4 TB HDD is far slower
  than a 256 GB SSD in practice. The correct picture: slowness usually means the processor
  is waiting on storage — the fix is a faster device (SSD) or more RAM (so less swapping
  of data between RAM and disk), not more room to fill. Contrasting example: two identical
  laptops, one with a 2 TB HDD and one with a 512 GB SSD — the smaller machine boots in
  seconds and opens programs instantly; the bigger one takes minutes, because it is
  mechanical, not because it is small.</p>

  <h2>Network hardware</h2>
  <p>
    The last topic is the hardware that lets machines talk. The <strong>NIC</strong>
    (network interface card) is the device's own connection point — every computer, phone
    and printer on a network has one, wired or wireless, and each carries a unique
    hardware address (the MAC address) burned in at manufacture. A <strong>hub</strong> is
    the simplest connector: it takes any incoming data and blindly repeats it out of every
    other port — every machine hears everything, collisions multiply, and the design is
    all but obsolete. A <strong>switch</strong> looks identical and behaves intelligently:
    it learns which MAC address lives on which port and forwards each frame only to its
    destination — no shouting, far higher throughput; modern LANs are built from switches.
    A <strong>bridge</strong> joins two network segments and filters traffic between them,
    so local chatter stays local. A <strong>repeater</strong> does one job: boosts a signal
    that has degraded over distance, extending the network's reach. A <strong>router</strong>
    connects <em>different networks</em> and forwards packets between them using IP
    addresses — your home router sits between your local network and the internet,
    choosing where each packet goes next. A <strong>gateway</strong> connects networks
    that speak different protocols, translating at the boundary (the term also appears in
    everyday use for any router-plus-translation box). A <strong>modem</strong>
    (modulator–demodulator) converts between a computer's digital signals and the analogue
    signals a telephone or cable line carries — in most homes it is packaged inside the
    same box as the router. The selection logic: same network, connect (switch);
    different network, route (router); different protocols, translate (gateway); weak
    signal, amplify (repeater).
  </p>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> "a switch and a hub do the same job — both just connect
  devices, so either will do." It seems reasonable because they look identical, share the
  same ports and sit in the same rack slot. But the behaviour differs at the wire level: a
  hub floods every bit of data to every port, so machines collide and waste time, while a
  switch addresses its deliveries — one frame, one port. The correct picture: a hub is a
  megaphone, a switch is a postal sorter; both "connect", but only one scales. The
  contrasting evidence: ten machines on a hub each get a small share of the bandwidth and
  degrade as more join; the same ten on a switch each get near full speed, because their
  traffic never meets.</p>

  <h2>Check yourself</h2>
  <p>Try these before moving on. Feedback follows each answer.</p>
  <ol>
    <li>During the fetch stage, name two registers and one bus that are used, and say what each carries.</li>
    <li>A colleague says: "My laptop has 16 GB of storage, so it must be fast." Untangle the two mistakes in that sentence.</li>
    <li>A office floor has 30 computers that must share a wired connection, and the building's own network runs on different equipment. Which devices are needed, and why not a hub?</li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    <strong>Feedback:</strong> (1) The program counter holds the address of the next
    instruction; that address travels down the address bus to memory; memory returns the
    instruction along the data bus into the memory data register, which passes it to the
    current instruction register — any two registers plus either bus, correctly described,
    earn the marks. (2) Mistake one: storage (gigabytes of permanent space on SSD/HDD) and
    RAM (the fast volatile workspace) are different things — "16 GB" could be either, and
    modern laptops typically have much more storage than that. Mistake two: capacity does
    not set speed; a machine feels fast because of processor, RAM sufficiency and storage
    type — a 16 GB SSD would be slower than a 512 GB SSD despite any size comparison.
    (3) A switch to connect the 30 computers (each frame delivered only to its
    destination port, no collisions) and a router to join the floor's network to the
    building's different network. A hub would broadcast every frame to all 30 machines,
    so shared bandwidth collapses and collisions multiply as the floor grows — the postal
    sorter beats the megaphone at this scale.
  </p>

  <h2>Key takeaways</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>The von Neumann architecture stores instructions and data in one memory — the stored-program concept that all modern computers share.</li>
    <li>The CPU runs one loop forever: fetch (PC, MAR, address bus, data bus, MDR, CIR), decode (control unit), execute (ALU and beyond).</li>
    <li>Buses have contracts: addresses one way (CPU to memory), data both ways, control signals from the CU.</li>
    <li>RAM is fast, read-write and volatile — the workspace; ROM is non-volatile and fixed — the bootstrap; secondary storage is large, permanent and slower.</li>
    <li>Input devices digitise people and environment (keyboards, 2D/3D scanners, barcode and QR readers, sensors); output devices materialise data (screens, laser/inkjet/3D printers, speakers, actuators).</li>
    <li>Storage trades capacity, speed, cost and reliability: HDD cheap and big, optical small and portable, SSD fast and rugged at a price.</li>
    <li>Network hardware has a grammar: NIC (connect), switch (deliver locally), bridge and repeater (extend), router (route between networks), gateway (translate), modem (convert).</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Where this goes next</h2>
  <p>
    The foundational lesson <em>Binary and Bits</em> supplies the number system every device
    in this lesson speaks — registers, buses and barcodes alike — and the intermediate
    lesson <em>Logic Gates and Digital Circuits</em> builds the CPU itself out of
    transistors, from AND and NAND to arithmetic and memory. <em>Programming
    Fundamentals</em> shows what the fetch–decode–execute loop looks like from the
    programmer's chair, and <em>Data Files, Tables and JSON</em> follows the storage topic
    into the formats files actually take.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    The von Neumann design has a known weakness with its own name: the <strong>von Neumann
    bottleneck</strong>. Since one memory holds both instructions and data, and one
    channel — the buses — serves it, the processor spends much of its life waiting for
    fetches: the loop's speed is limited not by how fast the ALU computes but by how fast
    instructions and data can travel in from memory. Forty years of processor engineering
    is a series of answers to that waiting. <strong>Cache memory</strong> places small,
    extremely fast memory between CPU and RAM and keeps there the instructions and data
    used most recently, betting (correctly, most of the time) that programs reuse what
    they just used. <strong>Pipelining</strong> overlaps the cycle's stages — while one
    instruction executes, the next is decoded and the one after is fetched, so the
    processor is an assembly line rather than a single queue. <strong>Multiple cores</strong>
    put several complete CPUs on one chip so independent streams of work run at once —
    which is why a "3 GHz, 8-core" phone chip is not three times faster than a 1 GHz
    single-core chip of the same generation, but often thirty times: more lanes, less
    waiting. Each refinement leaves the fetch–decode–execute skeleton intact — the loop
    you learned is still the machine's heartbeat; engineering only hides the waiting.
  </p>
  <p>
    A second depth worth carrying forward is that the syllabus's four topics are one story
    about bandwidth. Input and output devices are the network between humans and silicon;
    storage is the trade-off between permanence and speed; network hardware is the same
    story between machines. The 3D scanner, the SSD and the switch are all answers to the
    same question — how fast can the right bits get to the right place — asked at three
    different scales, and each field's current frontier is the same chase: scanners reading
    geometry in real time, storage replacing trapped-charge flash with faster memories,
    switches forwarding packets in hardware rather than software. Learn the four topics as
    one bandwidth story and the exam's device lists stop being lists.
  </p>

